data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/home/<USER:1>/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/report.md\", \"content\": \"# Campaign report — greet, tested\\n\\n## Mission\\nDeliver `src/greet.py` with `greet(name)` returning `\\\"Hello, \u003cname\u003e!\\\"` and\\n`tests/test_greet.py` covering a normal name and the empty string, with\\n`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exiting 0.\\n\\n## What happened\\n- Dispatched d002 to `dev` with the exact mission requirements.\\n- `dev` implemented `src/greet.py` and `tests/test_greet.py` and committed to\\n  `cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>` (commit `29ca8f4`, base\\n  `1053761f`).\\n- Judged independently rather than trusting the reply: fetched the branch,\\n  checked out a detached worktree from `campaign/dev/subject`, inspected both\\n  files, and re-ran `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` myself.\\n\\n## Verification (orchestrator-run, independent of dev's report)\\n- `src/greet.py`:\\n  ```python\\n  def greet(name):\\n      return \\\"Hello, {}!\\\".format(name)\\n  ```\\n- `tests/test_greet.py` covers `greet(\\\"World\\\")` == `\\\"Hello, World!\\\"` and\\n  `greet(\\\"\\\")` == `\\\"Hello, !\\\"`.\\n- `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` → `Ran 2 tests ... OK`, exit code 0.\\n\\n## Outcome\\nAll three stated criteria are met. dev's reply (d002) was accepted.\\nCampaign outcome: **campaign-met**.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":1,"type":"content_block_delta"}

